## Summary

This change scopes the order applicable for conventional and dynamic routes to the router context in which they are defined.

This change scopes conventional and dynamic routes only to the router context wehre they are defined.

## Motivation

Currently when a developer is using MapControllerRoute across pipeline branches, the route used for matching is defined globally, which causes additional endpoints to be mapped and additional (unexpected routes to be matched).

One example of this is:
```csharp
app.Map("/path1", builder =>
{
    builder.UseRouting();
    builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
            "Route1", "route1",
            defaults: new { controller = "WeatherForecast", action = "Action1" });
    });
});

app.Map("/path2", builder =>
{
    builder.UseRouting();
    builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
            "Route2", "route2",
            defaults: new { controller = "WeatherForecast", action = "Action2" });
    });
});
```

The result of this is that a request for `/path1/route2` will be matched, when it shouldn't.

As described in https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/issues/19330.

Additionally, another subtle effect of this is that the route will not have the correct order applied to it.
```csharp
endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
    "Route2", "route2",
    defaults: new { controller = "WeatherForecast", action = "Action2" });
```

In this case, the order is 2, when it should be 1. This can cause hard to debug issues when used in combination with other routes, since for a similar route definition, a request can match an endpoint when the router is defined in one branch and match a different endpoint when the router is defined in another branch. For example:

```csharp
app.Map("/path1", builder =>
{
    builder.UseRouting();
    builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapControllers();
        endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
            "hello/{template}",
            defaults: new { controller = "Sample", action = "Action1" });
    });
});

app.Map("/path2", builder =>
{
    builder.UseRouting();
    builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapControllers();
        endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
            "hello/{template}",
            defaults: new { controller = "Other", action = "Action2" });
    });
});
```
and an attributed route controller like the following:

```csharp
public class MyEndpointController : Controller
{
  [HttpGet("/{attribute}/{route}", Order = 1)]
  public IActionResult MyEndpoint()
  {
    return ...
  }
}
```

In this case, a request like `/path1/hello/world` will select `Action1` over `MyEndpoint` since the order is the same but the controller route has higher precedence and a request like `/path2/hello/world` will select `MyEndpoint` instead of `Action2` since the order the current route gets is 2 instead of 1, which is not expected.

The problem is made worse by the fact that we also introduced order in dynamic controller/page mappings.

## Goals
* Make order for conventional and dynamic routes work on a `per-router` basis.
* Make sure conventional routes are defined on a `per-router` basis for matching purposes.
* Maintain compatibility with link generation.
## Non-goals
* Link generation support for dynamic endpoints.
* Improved link generation support across different route branches.
  * Link generation is global, while route matching is specific to each router middleware. We don't aim to make to improve the current situation for link generation across branches. That is, your endpoints need to be unambiguous across branches (globally) for link generation. As an example of this:
  ```csharp
  app.Map("/path1", builder =>
  {
      builder.UseRouting();
      builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
      {
          endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
              "hello/{controller}/{action}",
              defaults: new { controller = "Sample", action = "Action1" });
      });
  });

  app.Map("/path2", builder =>
  {
      builder.UseRouting();
      builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
      {
          endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
              "world/{controller}/{action}",
              defaults: new { controller = "Other", action = "Action2" });
      });
  });
  ```
  Is ambiguous unless the values for the controller or action are provided (or route name is specified), since order doesn't play a role in link generation and the two routes would be valid candidates in that case.

## Scenarios

* As a developer I want to use routing in multiple branches:
  ```csharp
  app.Map("v1", builder =>
  {
      builder.UseRouting();
      
      builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
      {
          endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
              "{controller}/{action}",
              defaults: new { Version = "v1" });
      });
  });

  app.Map("/path2", builder =>
  {
      builder.UseRouting();
      builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
      {
          endpoints.MapControllerRoute("{version}/{controller}/{action}");
      });
  });
  ```

* As a developer I want to use routing in multiple branches:
  ```csharp
  app.Map("v1", builder =>
  {
      builder.UseRouting();
      
      builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
      {
          endpoints.MapControllerRoute(
              "{controller}/{action}",
              defaults: new { Version = "v1" });
      });
  });

  builder.UseRouting();
  builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
  {
      endpoints.MapControllerRoute("{version}/{controller}/{action}");
  });
  ```

* As a developer I want consistent order definition for routes on each branch:
  ```csharp
  app.Map("/common", builder =>
  {
      // Middleware and services specific to a common tenant
      builder.UseRouting();

      builder.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
      {
        endpoints.MapPages();
        endpoints.MapControllerRoute("common/{controller}/{action}", defaults: new { tenant = "common" }); // Order 1
        endpoints.MapControllerRoute("common/metadata", defaults: new { controller = "metatada", action = "index", tenant = "common" }); // Order 2
        endpoints.MapDynamicControllerRoute<MyTransformer>("common/{**slug}", ...); // Order 3
      });
  });


  app..UseRouting();
      
  app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
  {
    endpoints.MapControllers();
    endpoints.MapControllerRoute("{tenant}/{controller}/{action}"); // Order 1
    endpoints.MapControllerRoute("metadata", defaults: new { controller = "metatada", action = "index" }); // Order 2
    endpoints.MapDynamicControllerRoute<MyTransformer>("{**slug}", ...); // Order 3
  });
  ```
* As a developer I don't want route definitions from one middlware branch to "bleed" into other branches.  

## Risks
* Interfering with link generation in unexpected ways, see below for a section describing the current status of link generation in these cases, the impact the current changes have on link generation, the guidance we can give customers in these cases and the alternatives we can look at for in the future.

### Current status for link generation
* There is no support for link generation for dynamic routes. (We don't plan to change that).
* The endpoints used for link generation are defined globally, so they need to be unambiguous globally.

### Impact on link generation for the current changes
* There's no support for link generation for dynamic routes, so no impact there.
* Conventional route endpoints will still be registered globally and need to be unique, so it doesn't make anything worse.

### Potential future improvements
* We could resolve some of the link generation ambiguities in a similar way we do with areas in MVC with a bit of help from the map helpers.
  * We can make `Map("/path",...)` add a value to the IApplicationBuilder properties and include that as a route value for the link generation endpoints defined in that branch.

## Interaction with other parts of the framework
* Link generation
* Dynamic route matching

## Detailed design
* Create a "per-router" Controller/Page endpoint data source with a unique "id" and save it into the route collection.
* Create a cache and register the data source on it when mapping dynamic routes, this is because the `DynamicControllerEndpointSelector` uses the `ControllerActionEndpointDataSource` as a dependency from DI and the `ControllerActionEndpointDataSource` needs to be scoped to the router the dynamic route is defined on.
* Assign a piece of metadata to each dynamic endpoint that can be used to create or retrieve the `DynamicControllerEndpointSelector` associated with that router instance.
* A similar reasoning applies to razor pages and dynamic routes.
* Create a cache based on the `IEndpointRouteBuilder` instances to store the order sequences for routes "per-router". Use that order sequence to establish an order that is `scoped` to the routes defined in `each router`.
* Register conventional routes globally to maintain compatibility with link generation (TBD).

## Drawbacks
* The implementation is more complex than the current one, but the behavior is more predictable and explainable, and doesn't interfere in a negative way with link generation.

## Considered alternatives
* Do nothing. This is always an option to consider :). We can choose to ignore the routes being registered globally, but this leads to a poor experience, since your actions/controllers can be hit in unexpected ways and that can have unknown consequences. Using conventional/dynamic routes becomes very confusing/unreliable across branches now that we've implement order in dynamic routes too. Adding a new branch or route in a given pipeline branch can cause the routes in your app to start matching differently and that's almost impossible to diagnose.

* Only fix order. This is doable and introduces minimal complexity on the system (we create a "per-router" cache for order sequences and pass the order explicitly to all conventional route helpers and dynamic routes). This at least gives a consistent order to conventional and dynamic routes that is based on the order in which they are being defined in code and for the branch they are on. This is straightforward and easy to explain to customers.

* Fix order and matching: This provides the best experience, endpoints match when you expect and the conventional routes are defined in a simple and explainable way.

